!id: YXPzEiGy44
!front: State the Archimedean property of \( \mathbb{R} \).
!back: For every \( x \in \mathbb{R} \), there exists \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) with \( n > x \).
~~~

!id: 9rDsI75K6G
!front: Derive the Archimedean property of \( \mathbb{R} \) from the completeness axiom.
!back:
Suppose, for contradiction, that \( \mathbb{N} \) is bounded above.

To prove: \( \mathbb{N} \) is unbounded above in \( \mathbb{R} \).

- By completeness, \( s = \sup \mathbb{N} \) exists.
- Since \( s - 1 < s \), \( s-1 \) is not an upper bound of \( \mathbb{N} \), so there exists \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) with \( n > s - 1 \).
- Then \( n + 1 \in \mathbb{N} \) satisfies \( n+1 > s \), contradicting that \( s \) is an upper bound of \( \mathbb{N} \).
- So \( \mathbb{N} \) is unbounded above, i.e. for every \( x \in \mathbb{R} \) some \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) exceeds it.
~~~

!id: 029LBMbKUD
!front: Derive the reciprocal form of the Archimedean property: for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \), there exists \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) with \( 1/n < \varepsilon \).
!back:
Let \( \varepsilon > 0 \) be given.

To prove: there exists \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) with \( 1/n < \varepsilon \).

- By the Archimedean property applied to \( x = 1/\varepsilon \), there exists \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) with \( n > 1/\varepsilon \).
- Since \( n > 0 \) and \( \varepsilon > 0 \), multiplying both sides of \( n > 1/\varepsilon \) by \( \varepsilon/n > 0 \) gives \( \varepsilon > 1/n \).
~~~
